<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Committee of Public Safety</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1793">1793</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Committee of Public Safety was the executive body set up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">French National Convention</a> in April 1793 to direct the war and suppress internal revolt. Dominated from July 1793 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre">Maximilien Robespierre</a> and his allies, it ran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a>, in which thousands of suspected enemies of the Revolution were executed. Its power collapsed after Robespierre's fall in July 1794, and it was abolished in 1795.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A widespread belief held that revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was in immediate peril, threatened by foreign armies, the revolts in the Vendée and foreign agents plotting from within. The Girondin leader Maximin Isnard proposed a nine-member committee, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-danton">Georges Danton</a>, who called it &quot;a hand to grasp the weapon of the Revolutionary Tribunal&quot;. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> created it on 6 April 1793, after a proposal by Bertrand Barère. It supplemented the Committee of General Defence of early January 1793, and at first it was called the Danton Committee. Danton was not included when it was recomposed on 10 July 1793.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre">Maximilien Robespierre</a> was elected to the committee on 27 July 1793. It governed the war, named the judges and juries of the Revolutionary Tribunal, provisioned the armies and the public and oversaw the state bureaucracy. It applied the Convention's decrees and so carried out some of the harshest policies of the Terror, among them the levée en masse of 23 August 1793 and the Law of Suspects of 17 September. The Law of 14 Frimaire on 4 December 1793 codified its powers.</p><p>The Hébertists were arrested and executed in March 1794, and Danton and Camille Desmoulins, who had turned the newspaper Le Vieux Cordelier against the committees, went to the guillotine on 5 April 1794. The Law of 22 Prairial, enacted on 10 June 1794, limited the legal recourse of the accused and made death the punishment for all the crimes it covered. Discord grew within the government, and Robespierre quarrelled with Collot d'Herbois and Billaud-Varenne and almost stopped attending in June 1794 because of illness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 26 July 1794 Robespierre told the Convention that the committees needed purifying. The next day, 9 Thermidor, Saint-Just was interrupted by Jean-Lambert Tallien and Billaud-Varenne, and the Convention ordered the arrest of Robespierre, his brother Augustin, Saint-Just and their supporters. Philippe Le Bas killed himself, and the others were guillotined on 28 July 1794. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermidorian-reaction">Thermidorian Reaction</a> that followed repealed many of the year's most unpopular laws and restricted the committees. The committee's influence diminished over 15 months, and it disappeared on 25 October 1795, the day the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> ended, under the Constitution of the Year III, which began the Directory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Committee of Public Safety” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Among the tasks it then gave the <b>Committee of Public Safety</b> were the food shortages and riots and the radical movement of the Enragés.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-first-republic">French First Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Oliver Cromwell’s Protectorate of 1653–1658 was described as a military dictatorship by his enemies and by some modern scholars, and Maximilien Robespierre was called a dictator while the <b>Committee of Public Safety</b> directed the Reign of Terror in France in 1793–1794.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Facing war and revolt, the Convention gave power to the <b>Committee of Public Safety</b> under Robespierre; the Terror executed thousands before his fall in July 1794.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-first-republic">French First Republic</a></li></ul></section>
